Subject: [PATCH] glassine: pixel trees, whole scales for whole-scale-only clients,
 no scaling by one pixel

An e-ink panel needs sharp pixels: no client buffer may be resampled.

- A scene tree can be a pixel tree (wlr_scene_tree_set_pixel_units): the
  positions and sizes below it count in units of n whole pixels of an output
  of a given scale, from the output pixel the tree's own layout position
  rounds to. A buffer of n pixels per unit is then drawn pixel for pixel
  wherever it sits in the tree, which whole layout coordinates cannot say at
  a fractional scale. Towards the rest of the scene the nodes have layout
  boxes rounded outwards (visibility, damage, box tests) and opaque regions
  rounded inwards; wlr_scene_node_at gives positions in units. A pixel tree
  can be clipped in output pixels (wlr_scene_tree_set_pixel_clip) and is
  never scanned out directly.
- At a fractional output scale, and below a pixel tree, a buffer whose size
  is within one pixel of its box in output pixels is drawn one to one with
  the nearest filter: a pixel too many is cut, a pixel too few repeats the
  last row or column.
- wl_output.scale and the preferred buffer scale of a surface without a
  fractional scale object are the nearest whole number to the output's scale
  (at least 1), not the next one up; below a pixel tree the tree's n. A
  surface with a fractional scale object keeps the next whole number up.
  wlr_fractional_scale_v1_surface_has_object tells the two apart.
- wlr_scene_node_for_each_buffer gives layout positions below a pixel tree;
  wlr_scene_node_get_layout_size gives a node's layout size.

At a whole output scale without pixel trees nothing changes.

